WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
Recently, tech CEOs have been saying

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:04.200
that we either achieved AGI or we're at

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.280
the verge of achieving AGI. And yet,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.680
nobody really agrees on what AGI really

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.640
is. One question worth pondering here is

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.160
whether AGI is even possible at all. And

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.720
this debate goes all the way back to

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.160
1980s, and Penrose stated that human

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.880
understanding is fundamentally

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.400
non-algorithmic and cannot be produced

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.960
by computer. And he came to this

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.640
conclusion by loosely applying

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.000
mathematical theorems from the 1930s.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.320
And when we look at how the current

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.080
model architecture looks like, way it

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.160
borrows terminologies from neuroscience,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.280
these are just loosely related terms,

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.160
but in reality, model architectures

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.080
today don't really resemble human

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.480
biology or human brain at all. So, I

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.360
think it's fair to say that at least, if

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.040
we really are close to achieving AGI,

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.240
AGI doesn't really require us to

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.200
reproduce the mechanism of human

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.800
intelligence. Now, going back to the

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.240
1980s, the framework that we often use

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.440
was this idea called weak AI versus

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.840
strong AI. Where weak AI is a narrow

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.600
intelligence, which is something that we

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.560
already have today. Machines today are

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.840
already doing faster and more optimized

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.880
tasks in narrow domains. And we also

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.000
have strong AI, which is fundamentally

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.000
different, where the actual definition

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.480
ties it closer to having a mind of its

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.840
own. But our public discourse around AGI

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.520
cares less about whether AGI will have a

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.800
mind or not in a philosophical sense,

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.920
but people today care more about how

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.160
capable these models are. So, instead of

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.120
using this older framework, though it's

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.640
really helpful to know, we'll look at it

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.880
in these two axes in our discussion on

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.280
AGI. Mechanism and capabilities.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.280
Mechanism referring to how intelligence

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.160
is acquired and updated, and

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.440
capabilities referring to the tasks that

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.320
it can demonstrate. And people will hop

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.360
back and forth between these two to

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.120
either prove or disprove that AGI has

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.920
already been reached. People argue that

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.160
large language models by mechanism are

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.480
nothing more than next token prediction.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.600
So, it cannot and does not fully

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.480
understand. On the other side, people

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.880
also say things like, "Well, Astra can't

00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.800
really do this task or Fable can't

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.720
really do that task on the capabilities

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.000
axis. And one prime example that really

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.080
embodies this is Y Ann LeCun. Y Ann

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.360
LeCun has long been arguing that LLMs

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.320
are dead, but he also has been critical

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.520
towards what LLMs can't really do in

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.280
terms of capabilities. Recently, he

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.200
showed up on my LinkedIn making comments

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.680
like this saying that current, quote

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.520
unquote, AGI can't really fix the

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.840
bathroom, help watch kids, or even drive

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.280
vehicles fully autonomously. So, as we

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.720
unpack this state of AGI, I'll be using

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.160
these two axes to ground our discussion.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.520
Now, let's start with the mechanics

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.480
behind AGI. In an interview with

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.200
François Chollet, he reasoned that we

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.600
haven't reached AGI yet. And his opinion

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.640
really boils down to whether or not the

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.160
model can acquire skills in novel

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.160
situations. The problem right now is

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.360
that models like Astra and Fable are

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.640
manufactured in the lab and trained in

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.720
the data center for months until a

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.680
checkpoint is released to the public.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.480
But, the intelligence that's currently

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.680
baked into the parameters of the models

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.480
are more or less immutable during

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.800
inference. So, by François's definition,

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.000
we haven't reached AGI yet. You see a

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.000
similar opinion being shown when we look

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.920
at the benchmarks that he created, ARC

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.120
AGI. ARC AGI tries to measure the

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.280
unspoken rule that the model needs to

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.000
learn on the fly. But, the reason why

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.160
models like Astra can reach upwards to

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.840
99.9% on ARC AGI 3 despite the model

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.519
still not really learning anything has a

00:03:20.519 --> 00:03:22.959
lot to do with the harness. Astra scored

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:24.600
very differently depending on which

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.280
harness that you used. So, while the

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.800
model doesn't actually learn new skill

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.200
during the ARC AGI 3 test, a lot of

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.400
learning is actually possible through

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.440
the harness that helps manage its own

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.720
context, help have longer memory,

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.560
provide an execution environment, and

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.680
help recover from errors. And these are

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.000
essential elements of intelligence that

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.920
we just don't see in the model layer

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.680
alone. But, when we put the model with

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.880
harnesses like Codex that provide

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.200
skills, MCP for databases, terminal, and

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.080
loops. We've essentially outsourced a

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.120
currently difficult task in the model

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.440
architecture to handwritten code

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.400
directly in the harness. So, while we

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.240
certainly can't say that the model by

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.880
itself has reached AGI just yet, we

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:07.560
could reasonably make a case that we're

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.880
seeing early signs of AGI when we look

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:12.280
at the entire system that includes both

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:14.320
the model and the harness that currently

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.200
acts as a stopgap. And this certainly

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.200
brings back to the famous bitter lessons

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.120
from Richard Sutton. Since the harness

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.040
layer right now is hand engineered by

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.880
writing rules and programs. And if the

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.919
bitter lesson is true, some of the

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:27.680
structure that currently exists in the

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:29.880
harness should get replaced with learned

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.280
behavior rather than being explicitly

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.840
programmed. And even looking at Claude

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.800
Code, where Sherny, who created Claude

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.400
Code, has labeled this harness layer as

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.880
a tradeoff where engineering work can

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.800
essentially extend the model's ability

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.880
by 10 to 20%. Another angle worth

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.480
considering here is when we look at the

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.080
capabilities access of AGI. And having a

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.560
capable AGI requires good computers,

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.000
which is why you have to shop at Micro

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.080
Center sponsoring this video. September

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.600
is all about build, upgrade, and save.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.120
And it's perfect time to actually

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:00.840
upgrade your PC at home that you might

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.480
have been putting off. As hardware

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.360
prices continue to climb, having a deal

